# phantomos-base — the explicit, no-GUI selection.
#
# Derived from docs/SPEC.md §5 on 2026-10-30. On any conflict, §5 wins and this
# file is corrected; nothing here is a choice made at this level.
# Format: one package name per line; '#' starts a comment; blank lines ignored.
# Versions are never pinned here — §5 pins none, and the archive is the authority
# (§3.1's fact table records what the archive actually holds, with a date).

# PhantomOS's own. Both are built by scripts/build-packages.sh from this repo;
# phantomos-config ships empty until M2 fills it (§10), and it is listed here because
# §5 names it in the base inventory — the M1 diff measures the inventory as written.
phantomos-release
phantomos-config

# §6 controls, in the order §6 lists them
sudo                      # §6.7 no root password, sudo with tty
unattended-upgrades       # §6.6
apparmor                  # §6.3
nftables                  # §6.5
lvm2                      # §6.1 ext4 on LVM
cryptsetup                # §6.1 LUKS2
ca-certificates           # §8 trust anchor for the signed channel
apt-listchanges           # §6.6 "no install path may silently never update"
openssh-client            # §5: client only — openssh-server is a drop, §6.5

# §5.1 sandboxed apps, with no remote preconfigured (§5.1, §6.8)
flatpak

# Hardware enablement, named because nothing else names them any more (§9.1).
#
# live-build's chroot_firmware stage builds its package list by reading the archive's Contents
# file and taking every package that ships a path under lib/firmware, then hands those names to
# apt as explicit installs. It reads Contents-all and Contents-<arch>, so against trixie main the
# rule yields six names — five from Contents-all (32 paths: alsa-topology-conf,
# firmware-ath9k-htc, firmware-carl9170, firmware-linux-free, wireless-regdb) plus indi-dsi from
# Contents-amd64 (1 path). logs/M1-firmware-contents-repro.txt has the reproduction and
# logs/M1-firmware-contents-lines.txt the Contents line for each. Five of the six are worth
# having; indi-dsi is a Meade DSLR camera driver that ships
# usr/lib/firmware/meade-deepskyimager.hex and is in the image only because of that path.
# Against trixie's non-free-firmware the same rule yields 34 packages over 4792 paths, and
# contrib 2 more (logs/M1-firmware-nonfree-scale.txt), so the rule is what has to go, not the
# list. The block below is the version of it that somebody read.

#
# So live/auto/config sets --firmware-chroot false and the wanted names are written here, each
# with the reason it is in the image. Nothing is added that the enumeration gave us for free and
# nobody checked. The sizes below are the .deb bytes apt fetched (logs/M1-firmware-contents-lines.txt §4).
linux-image-amd64           # §6.4: Debian's kernel as shipped, no rebuild. live-build's own
                            # chroot_linux-image stage names it too; naming it here is what puts
                            # it in the inventory instead of in the drift column
firmware-linux-free         # 17832 B, 24 lib/firmware paths — the blobs in-kernel drivers request
firmware-ath9k-htc          # 45040 B — AR7010/AR9271 USB Wi-Fi, i.e. the adapter a friend brings
firmware-carl9170           # 15336 B — AR9170 USB Wi-Fi, same reason
wireless-regdb              # 12400 B — regulatory.db, without which the kernel cannot set
                            # country-correct transmit power on Wi-Fi. §12 item 12 lists it as a
                            # Recommends of network-manager, and --apt-recommends false turns that
                            # route off, so naming it here is what keeps it. It is also the only
                            # one of the five with a third-party edge in the archive: nothing
                            # Depends or Pre-Depends on it but reform-desktop-minimal, which this
                            # selection does not pull (logs/M1-firmware-ownership.txt)
alsa-topology-conf          # 15276 B — ships usr/lib/firmware/skl_hda_dsp_generic-tplg.bin, the
                            # Intel Skylake+ HDA/DSP topology file. The only machine this repo
                            # knows about is §9's build host, an HP EliteBook 840 G3 (i5-6200U,
                            # Skylake); no friend's hardware has been recorded anywhere
